# Which sent more into space: one year, or the whole space age?
subtitle: "Tap the bigger of three space-age pairs, then watch sixty flat years of launches shoot up, and meet the one project behind most of it."
tags: ["astronomy","data","history of science"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"One year vs the space age\" note \"Guess first\""

## Which sent more into space: one year, or the whole space age?
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1957
      - 1960
      - 1965
      - 1970
      - 1975
      - 1980
      - 1985
      - 1990
      - 1995
      - 2000
      - 2005
      - 2010
      - 2015
      - 2017
      - 2018
      - 2019
      - 2020
      - 2021
      - 2022
      - 2023
      - 2024
      - 2025
    series:
      - {name: Launches, values: [2, 20, 161, 130, 158, 130, 165, 170, 105, 121, 72, 121, 222, 456, 452, 586, 1274, 1814, 2479, 2903, 2849, 4510]}
    xlim:
      - 1955
      - 2027
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 5000
    y_label: Objects launched per year
    label_size: 40
sources:
    - id: owid
      title: Yearly number of objects launched into outer space
      authors: Our World in Data (Edouard Mathieu, Pablo Rosado), from the United Nations Office for Outer Space Affairs
      year: 2026
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/yearly-number-of-objects-launched-into-outer-space
      finding: 4,510 objects launched in 2025; 1,080 in 1957–1969; 5,408 in 1957–2000; 15,829 in 2020–2025 against 9,036 in 1957–2019 (24,865 in all)
    - id: insight
      title: A record number of objects went into space
      authors: Edouard Mathieu and Pablo Rosado, Our World in Data
      year: 2026
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/data-insights/a-record-number-of-objects-went-into-space-in-2023
      finding: 4,510 objects launched in 2025, far above the 2023 peak of 2,903; the US 82 %; driven by commercial constellations, raising concerns about debris and congestion
    - id: mcdowell
      title: Space Activities in 2025
      authors: Jonathan McDowell
      year: 2026
      url: https://planet4589.org/space/papers/space25.pdf
      finding: SpaceX launched more than 3,000 Starlink satellites in 2025

    cards duel cites ["owid"] size 1.8 y 540 unit objects compare "Which sent more into space?" hidden true
        * "2025 alone" icon.of rocket icon.set twemoji value 4510
        * "1957–1969" icon.of "crescent moon" icon.set twemoji value 1080
        * "2025 alone" icon.of rocket icon.set twemoji value 4510
        * "1957–2000" icon.of satellite icon.set twemoji value 5408
        * "2020–2025" icon.of rocket icon.set twemoji value 15829
        * "1957–2019" icon.of satellite icon.set twemoji value 9036
    text flat_note "~100–200 a year" #2f6b8f at.data [1985,1700] cites ["owid"] font_size 44 hidden true
    dot endpt #b5482e at.data [2025,4510] hidden true
    text end "4,510" #b5482e at.data [2013,4500] cites ["owid"] font_size 60 hidden true
    icon sat at.data [1975,3600] size 140 set twemoji icon_look picture of satellite hidden true
    text star "Starlink: 3,000+" #b5482e at.data [1999,3600] cites ["mcdowell"] font_size 48 hidden true
    text share "64 % since 2020" #b5482e at.data [2004,2700] cites ["owid"] font_size 52 hidden true
    polygon since #f2c14e points [[2019.5,0],[2025.8,0],[2025.8,4900],[2019.5,4900]] data true style.fill #f2c14e style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.35 hidden true

    card title "Which sent more into space?"

A: Which sent more objects into space: one recent year, or whole decades of the space age?
    draw duel

    ask "Tap the bigger one in each pair." on duel store s feedback.style dry feedback.perfect "Mission control would hire you." feedback.good "A smooth launch, mostly." feedback.poor "Space fooled you. It fools most people." feedback.none "Houston, we have a problem."
        right: All {s} right. One year beats the whole Space Race, not the first forty-four years, and the last six beat everything before.
        wrong: {s.within} of {s.total} on the first try.

B: Hold on. One year beats the whole race to the Moon?
    highlight duel_1 duel_4 duel_5

    erase duel

A: It does. Here is every year since Sputnik: for almost sixty years, a hundred or two a year.
    draw axes line_1 flat_note

A: Then it took off: fo (@draw endpt end@)ur thousand five hundred and ten in 2025 alone.

    erase flat_note

A: But add up the forty-four calm years to 2000, and they still edge it: about five thousand four hundred.
    polygon old_area #2f6b8f points [[1957,2],[1960,20],[1965,161],[1970,130],[1975,158],[1980,130],[1985,165],[1990,170],[1995,105],[2000,121],[2000,0],[1957,0]] data true style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.5
    polygon col #b5482e points [[2024.4,0],[2025.6,0],[2025.6,4510],[2024.4,4510]] data true style.fill #b5482e style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.5
    text old "1957–2000, added up: 5,408" #2f6b8f at.data [1980,1000] cites ["owid"] font_size 46

B: So the old days win on stamina, not speed.
    highlight old_area

    erase old_area col old

A: What changed is mostly one company: in 2025, SpaceX put up more than three thousand Starlink internet satellites.
    draw sat star

B: One company, in one year, nearly three times the whole Space Race?
    highlight star

A: Yes. And nearly two thirds of everything ever launched went up since 2020.
    draw since share

B: Internet in the middle of nowhere, and traffic jams in orbit. Lovely.
    highlight since share

